                                ;============================================================
                                ; SEARCH  (/ ? n N)
                                ;
                                ; vi's search on WordMaster's matcher.  What it is NOT: vim searches with a
                                ; REGULAR EXPRESSION and this searches for the literal string typed.  An 8080
                                ; has no room for a regex engine; COMMANDS.md records the deviation.  (WM's own
                                ; search wildcards -- ^A any, ^S separator, ^O not -- still reach WCMATCH from
                                ; here, because MATCHF/MATCHB compare through it, but they are WordMaster's
                                ; syntax, not vim's, and nothing advertises them.)
                                ;
                                ; Recorded from vim before any of this was written (VIM_SRCH, accept_vi.py):
                                ;   - '/' starts one character PAST the cursor and lands ON the match's first
                                ;     character; '?' finds the last match that ends before the cursor.
                                ;   - 'wrapscan' is on: a search that runs off one end of the file starts
                                ;     again at the other, so '/' on the file's ONLY match finds it again.
                                ;   - 'n' repeats the last search's DIRECTION and 'N' reverses it, so 'n'
                                ;     after a '?' keeps going backward.
                                ;   - An empty pattern ('/' then <CR>) repeats the last pattern.
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-108


                                ;   - A count repeats the search: '2/a' is the second match, '15n' fifteen
                                ;     n's.  Not found leaves the cursor exactly where it was, at any count.
                                ;   - The window is placed as 'G' places it -- least scroll when the match is
                                ;     near, centred when it is far -- which is why this ends in GPLACE.
                                ;
                                ; This replaces a byte-at-a-time search that ran through CHARAT over logical
                                ; offsets: it could only see the resident window (so a match further into a
                                ; paged file did not exist), had no '?' or 'N', and failed silently.
                                ;============================================================
                                
                                ;============================================================
                                ; SUBSTITUTE  (:s  :%s  :N,Ms  and the 'g' flag)
                                ;
                                ; vi's substitute on WordMaster's matcher, so it finds the same LITERAL string
                                ; the search does -- not a regular expression.  COMMANDS.md records that
                                ; deviation for '/' and it is the same one here, which is why nothing below
                                ; honours '&', '\1' or '~'.  The pattern goes into the SEARCH's own buffer
                                ; because vim's ':s' also sets the last search pattern, so 'n' afterwards
                                ; repeats it.
                                ;
                                ; Recorded from vim before any of this was written (VIM_SUBST, accept_vi.py):
                                ;   - ':s' changes the FIRST match on the cursor's line, ':s...g' every match,
                                ;     and the scan goes on PAST the replacement, so a replacement that
                                ;     contains the pattern is not substituted again.
                                ;   - the cursor lands on the first non-blank of the LAST line changed, placed
                                ;     exactly as 'G' places a line -- which is what GZONE before and GPLACE
                                ;     after are for, and it is measured: ':12800,12800s' leaves the last line
                                ;     on row 22 (top 012778) and ':100,105s' leaves line 105 on row 11.
                                ;   - a pattern that is nowhere in the range changes nothing and moves nothing.
                                ;
                                ; A whole substitute is ONE change for 'u': the second UNDBEG, at the first
                                ; match, is what makes the undo's cursor the first match (where vim leaves it)
                                ; rather than the line the ':' was typed on.  A substitute of more than about a
                                ; dozen lines overruns the undo region, which is accepted -- but it SAYS so,
                                ; both when it happens and when 'u' is then pressed.
                                ;============================================================
                                
                                ;EXADDR -- the optional line range before an ex command: '%' (the whole file),
                                ;'N', or 'N,M'.  SRNGF = 0 when there is none, and SRNG2 = 0FFFFH is the last
                                ;line, whatever it turns out to be.  HL comes back on the command letter.
                                ;
                                ; It does NOT consume the blanks after itself: VC_EX's SKIPB does that, so
                                ; ':2,4 s/a/Z/' reads as ':2,4s/a/Z/' the way ex has always taken it.  The one
                                ; place a blank is still refused is INSIDE the range, around the comma
                                ; (':2, 4s'), which ex accepts and this does not -- the comma is read here off
                                ; DE mid-number and skipping there costs more than the position is worth.
  235A'                         EXADDR:
  235A'   AF                    	XRA	A
  235B'   32 29B1'              	STA	SRNGF
  235E'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  235F'   FE 25                 	CPI	'%'
  2361'   CA 238A'              	JZ	EXA_AL
  2364'   EB                    	XCHG			; DE walks the line, HL takes the number
  2365'   CD 239F'              	CALL	EXNUM
  2368'   DA 2388'              	JC	EXA_NO
  236B'   22 29B2'              	SHLD	SRNG1
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-109


  236E'   22 29B4'              	SHLD	SRNG2		; 'N' alone is the single line 'N,N'
  2371'   1A                    	LDAX	D
  2372'   FE 2C                 	CPI	','
  2374'   C2 2381'              	JNZ	EXA_YS
  2377'   13                    	INX	D
  2378'   CD 239F'              	CALL	EXNUM
  237B'   DA 2388'              	JC	EXA_NO		; ',' with no number: not a range at all
  237E'   22 29B4'              	SHLD	SRNG2
  2381'                         EXA_YS:
  2381'   EB                    	XCHG
  2382'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  2384'   32 29B1'              	STA	SRNGF
  2387'   C9                    	RET
  2388'                         EXA_NO:
  2388'   EB                    	XCHG
  2389'   C9                    	RET
  238A'                         EXA_AL:
  238A'   23                    	INX	H
  238B'   E5                    	PUSH	H
  238C'   21 0001               	LXI	H,1
  238F'   22 29B2'              	SHLD	SRNG1
  2392'   21 FFFF               	LXI	H,0FFFFH
  2395'   22 29B4'              	SHLD	SRNG2
  2398'   E1                    	POP	H
  2399'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  239B'   32 29B1'              	STA	SRNGF
  239E'   C9                    	RET
                                
                                ;EXNUM -- a decimal number at DE -> HL, DE left past it.  CY when there is no
                                ;digit there at all.
  239F'                         EXNUM:
  239F'   1A                    	LDAX	D
  23A0'   D6 30                 	SUI	'0'
  23A2'   D8                    	RC
  23A3'   FE 0A                 	CPI	10
  23A5'   3F                    	CMC
  23A6'   D8                    	RC
  23A7'   21 0000               	LXI	H,0
  23AA'                         EXN_LP:
  23AA'   1A                    	LDAX	D
  23AB'   D6 30                 	SUI	'0'
  23AD'   DA 23C7'              	JC	EXN_DN
  23B0'   FE 0A                 	CPI	10
  23B2'   D2 23C7'              	JNC	EXN_DN
  23B5'   13                    	INX	D
  23B6'   C5                    	PUSH	B
  23B7'   4F                    	MOV	C,A
  23B8'   06 00                 	MVI	B,0		; BC = the digit
  23BA'   D5                    	PUSH	D
  23BB'   54                    	MOV	D,H
  23BC'   5D                    	MOV	E,L
  23BD'   29                    	DAD	H		; *2
  23BE'   29                    	DAD	H		; *4
  23BF'   19                    	DAD	D		; *5
  23C0'   29                    	DAD	H		; *10
  23C1'   D1                    	POP	D
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-110


  23C2'   09                    	DAD	B
  23C3'   C1                    	POP	B
  23C4'   C3 23AA'              	JMP	EXN_LP
  23C7'                         EXN_DN:
  23C7'   B7                    	ORA	A		; CY clear: there was a number
  23C8'   C9                    	RET
                                
                                ;EX_S -- ':s': the delimiter, the pattern, the replacement, an optional 'g'.
  23C9'                         EX_S:
  23C9'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  23CA'   B7                    	ORA	A
  23CB'   CA 240D'              	JZ	SUBNOP		; ':s' alone -- repeating the last one is not
                                				;   built (COMMANDS.md)
  23CE'   32 29B6'              	STA	SDELIM
  23D1'   23                    	INX	H
  23D2'   11 0000*              	LXI	D,SRCHST
  23D5'   3A 0000*              	LDA	SRCMAX		; the pattern's capacity is BUF.MAC's to state
  23D8'   4F                    	MOV	C,A
  23D9'   CD 2413'              	CALL	SCOPY
  23DC'   78                    	MOV	A,B
  23DD'   B7                    	ORA	A
  23DE'   CA 240D'              	JZ	SUBNOP		; ':s//x/' -- an empty pattern matches nothing
  23E1'   32 0000*              	STA	SRCHBF		; WM's RDSTR layout: the length below the string
  23E4'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  23E6'   32 2980'              	STA	SHAVE		; 'n' repeats it, as after a '/'
  23E9'   11 29C8'              	LXI	D,SREPL
  23EC'   0E 24                 	MVI	C,SREPMX
  23EE'   CD 2413'              	CALL	SCOPY
  23F1'   78                    	MOV	A,B
  23F2'   32 29C7'              	STA	SREPLN
  23F5'   AF                    	XRA	A
  23F6'   32 29B7'              	STA	SGFLAG
  23F9'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  23FA'   FE 67                 	CPI	'g'
  23FC'   C2 2405'              	JNZ	EX_SG
  23FF'   23                    	INX	H
  2400'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  2402'   32 29B7'              	STA	SGFLAG
  2405'                         EX_SG:
  2405'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  2406'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2407'   C2 21B0'              	JNZ	EX_TRL		; 'Trailing chars: ' -- vim's, shortened
  240A'   C3 2433'              	JMP	SUBGO
  240D'                         SUBNOP:
  240D'   21 225C'              	LXI	H,SNOPAT
  2410'   C3 21BE'              	JMP	EXERR
                                
                                ;SCOPY -- the text at HL up to SDELIM (or the line's end) into [DE], C its
                                ;capacity: B = how many were taken, HL past the delimiter, and [DE] is left
                                ;0-terminated as WM's RDSTR leaves a string.
  2413'                         SCOPY:
  2413'   06 00                 	MVI	B,0
  2415'                         SCP_LP:
  2415'   78                    	MOV	A,B
  2416'   B9                    	CMP	C
  2417'   D2 2430'              	JNC	SCP_DN		; full: what is left stays on the line, and so
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-111


                                				;   comes back as trailing characters
  241A'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  241B'   B7                    	ORA	A
  241C'   CA 2430'              	JZ	SCP_DN		; the line ended: no closing delimiter wanted
  241F'   E5                    	PUSH	H
  2420'   21 29B6'              	LXI	H,SDELIM
  2423'   BE                    	CMP	M
  2424'   E1                    	POP	H
  2425'   CA 242F'              	JZ	SCP_SK
  2428'   12                    	STAX	D
  2429'   13                    	INX	D
  242A'   23                    	INX	H
  242B'   04                    	INR	B
  242C'   C3 2415'              	JMP	SCP_LP
  242F'                         SCP_SK:
  242F'   23                    	INX	H
  2430'                         SCP_DN:
  2430'   AF                    	XRA	A
  2431'   12                    	STAX	D
  2432'   C9                    	RET
                                
                                ;SUBGO -- run the substitute.  GZONE comes first, before anything moves: the
                                ;landing is placed as 'G' places a line, and GPLACE measures it against the
                                ;screen's top line as it was when the command was typed.
  2433'                         SUBGO:
  2433'   CD 0EDF'              	CALL	GZONE
  2436'   2A 0000*              	LHLD	LNDLT
  2439'   22 29C5'              	SHLD	SLDLTS		; the walk moves the cursor; a substitute that
  243C'   21 29C2'              	LXI	H,SOFFS		;   finds nothing puts both back, as the
  243F'   CD 0442'              	CALL	FPSTO		;   search's SN_BAD does
  2442'   AF                    	XRA	A
  2443'   32 29B8'              	STA	SANY
  2446'   21 0000               	LXI	H,0
  2449'   22 29BA'              	SHLD	SBACK
  244C'   3A 29B1'              	LDA	SRNGF
  244F'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2450'   CA 2462'              	JZ	SUB_1L
                                	;--- ':N,Ms' / ':%s': to the range's first line, then walk it ---
  2453'   CD 1D3A'              	CALL	EXRNG		; SLINES = the lines in the range
  2456'   DA 24DE'              	JC	SUBBAD
  2459'   2A 29B2'              	LHLD	SRNG1
  245C'   CD 0E50'              	CALL	GOLN
  245F'   C3 2468'              	JMP	SUBLP
  2462'                         SUB_1L:
  2462'   21 0001               	LXI	H,1
  2465'   22 29BC'              	SHLD	SLINES
  2468'                         SUBLP:
  2468'   CD 24E4'              	CALL	SUBLIN		; A = 1 when this line changed
  246B'   B7                    	ORA	A
  246C'   CA 247A'              	JZ	SUB_NX
  246F'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  2471'   32 29B8'              	STA	SANY
  2474'   21 0000               	LXI	H,0
  2477'   22 29BA'              	SHLD	SBACK		; the cursor is ON the last line changed
  247A'                         SUB_NX:
  247A'   2A 29BC'              	LHLD	SLINES
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-112


  247D'   2B                    	DCX	H
  247E'   22 29BC'              	SHLD	SLINES
  2481'   7C                    	MOV	A,H
  2482'   B5                    	ORA	L
  2483'   CA 24A1'              	JZ	SUBDON		; the range is spent
  2486'   21 0001               	LXI	H,1
  2489'   AF                    	XRA	A
  248A'   CD 0000*              	CALL	MVLIND		; the next line, paging with it
  248D'   3A 29B8'              	LDA	SANY
  2490'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2491'   CA 249B'              	JZ	SUB_AE		; nothing changed yet: nothing to count from
  2494'   2A 29BA'              	LHLD	SBACK
  2497'   23                    	INX	H
  2498'   22 29BA'              	SHLD	SBACK		; every step taken after the last change has
                                				;   to be walked back at the end -- including
                                				;   this one, when it lands past the last line
  249B'                         SUB_AE:
  249B'   CD 0995'              	CALL	ATEOF
  249E'   D2 2468'              	JNC	SUBLP		; ... unless that ran off the end of the file
  24A1'                         SUBDON:
  24A1'   CD 0000*              	CALL	CLRCNT		; the walk left the count and direction set
  24A4'   3A 29B8'              	LDA	SANY
  24A7'   B7                    	ORA	A
  24A8'   CA 24C6'              	JZ	SUBNFD
  24AB'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  24AD'   32 2901'              	STA	MODF		; the text changed, so ':w' has work to do.  NOT
                                				;   MARKMOD: that also sets DOTCHG, and '.' does
                                				;   not repeat a substitute in vim either (that
                                				;   is '&', which is not built)
  24B0'   2A 29BA'              	LHLD	SBACK
  24B3'   7C                    	MOV	A,H
  24B4'   B5                    	ORA	L
  24B5'   CA 24C0'              	JZ	SUB_PL
  24B8'   3E FF                 	MVI	A,0FFH
  24BA'   CD 0000*              	CALL	MVLIND		; back to the last line changed
  24BD'   CD 0000*              	CALL	CLRCNT
  24C0'                         SUB_PL:
  24C0'   CD 0EF7'              	CALL	GPLACE		; the landing: first non-blank, placed as 'G'
  24C3'   C3 070C'              	JMP	SETFUL		; GPLACE asks for R_MOVE when the target is
                                				;   which rewrote the line under the cursor.
                                				; And that repaint is why a substitute too big
                                				;   for the undo does NOT say so here: EXMSG
                                				;   draws the bottom row there and then, and
                                				;   the full paint this needs takes the status
                                				;   row back (SCRN.MAC says so itself).  'u'
                                				;   carries the message instead -- UNDWHY = 2,
                                				;   at the moment the reader asks for the undo
                                				;   and can still act on the answer.
  24C6'                         SUBNFD:
  24C6'   21 29C2'              	LXI	H,SOFFS
  24C9'   CD 03E9'              	CALL	BACKTO
  24CC'   2A 29C5'              	LHLD	SLDLTS
  24CF'   22 0000*              	SHLD	LNDLT		; nothing changed: the row the cursor was on
  24D2'   21 2248'              	LXI	H,SNOTFD
  24D5'   CD 21BE'              	CALL	EXERR
  24D8'   21 0000*              	LXI	H,SRCHST
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-113


  24DB'   C3 21B9'              	JMP	EXOUT		; 'Pattern not found: ' and the pattern
  24DE'                         SUBBAD:
  24DE'   21 2236'              	LXI	H,SNOT
  24E1'   C3 21B3'              	JMP	EXERT
                                
                                ;SUBLIN -- the substitute on the cursor's line: the first match, or every match
                                ;when 'g' was given.  A = 1 when the line changed.  The cursor is left at the
                                ;line's start either way, so the walk to the next line always starts from one.
                                ;Every offset is re-derived from the cursor after each change, never held
                                ;across one: an insert can page (':%s/0/ZZ/' grows a 100 K file by 12800
                                ;bytes) and paging shifts every logical offset in the window.
  24E4'                         SUBLIN:
  24E4'   AF                    	XRA	A
  24E5'   32 29B9'              	STA	SLCHG
  24E8'   CD 2563'              	CALL	SUBBOL
  24EB'   CD 0000*              	CALL	RGET0		; the line has to be RESIDENT before CEND can
  24EE'                         SUBL_R:				;   find its end: the pager keeps the CURSOR in
  24EE'   CD 0000*              	CALL	RGET		;   the window, not the whole line it sits on,
  24F1'   DA 24F9'              	JC	SUBL_L		;   and EOLNL only ever scans resident text --
  24F4'   FE 0A                 	CPI	LF		;   so a line straddling the resident end came
  24F6'   C2 24EE'              	JNZ	SUBL_R		;   out short and went unsubstituted (line 179
                                				;   of the 66 K indented file, which starts at
                                				;   byte 2047).  RGET pages through NEEDIN, as
                                				;   every other line reader here does.
  24F9'                         SUBL_L:
                                	;--- the scan window: the cursor to the line's content end ---
  24F9'   CD 0000*              	CALL	CUROFF
  24FC'   E5                    	PUSH	H
  24FD'   CD 13F3'              	CALL	CEND
  2500'   D1                    	POP	D
  2501'   CD 0000*              	CALL	SUBHD		; HL = the bytes left on the line
  2504'   23                    	INX	H		; FWDCNT's own arithmetic, inline: the span it
  2505'   CD 0000*              	CALL	SUBLEN		;   would measure, plus one, less the pattern.
  2508'   7C                    	MOV	A,H		;   (FWDCNT needs a real pointer pair and this
  2509'   B7                    	ORA	A		;   span is a line, not a pair.)
  250A'   FA 255C'              	JM	SUBL_D		; what is left is shorter than the pattern
  250D'   44                    	MOV	B,H
  250E'   4D                    	MOV	C,L		; BC = the start positions a match can take
  250F'   11 0000*              	LXI	D,SRCHST
  2512'   2A 0000*              	LHLD	GAPEND		; with the gap at the cursor, the line runs
  2515'   CD 0000*              	CALL	MATCHF		;   forward from GAPEND
  2518'   DA 255C'              	JC	SUBL_D
  251B'   CD 0000*              	CALL	SUBLEN		; MATCHF ends past the match; back to its start
  251E'   EB                    	XCHG
  251F'   2A 0000*              	LHLD	GAPEND
  2522'   EB                    	XCHG
  2523'   CD 0000*              	CALL	SUBHD		; HL = how far into the scan the match is
  2526'   EB                    	XCHG
  2527'   D5                    	PUSH	D		; CUROFF does NOT keep DE (it reads TXTBEG into
  2528'   CD 0000*              	CALL	CUROFF		;   it), so the delta goes on the stack
  252B'   D1                    	POP	D
  252C'   19                    	DAD	D		; ... and so the match's logical offset
  252D'   CD 0000*              	CALL	GOTO
                                	;--- the first change of the whole substitute re-arms the undo, so 'u'
                                	;    lands on the first match, which is where vim leaves it ---
  2530'   3A 29B8'              	LDA	SANY
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-114


  2533'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2534'   C2 2541'              	JNZ	SUBL_C
  2537'   3A 29B9'              	LDA	SLCHG
  253A'   B7                    	ORA	A
  253B'   C2 2541'              	JNZ	SUBL_C
  253E'   CD 0000*              	CALL	UNDHER		; the cursor snapshot only: UNDBEG would re-run
                                				;   UNDGRW mid-command, and growing the region
                                				;   where a primitive can see it corrupts the
                                				;   gap ('COPY ERR')
  2541'                         SUBL_C:
  2541'   3A 0000*              	LDA	SRCHBF
  2544'   6F                    	MOV	L,A
  2545'   26 00                 	MVI	H,0
  2547'   CD 0000*              	CALL	DELFWD		; the match goes
  254A'   11 29C7'              	LXI	D,SREPLN	; ... and the replacement takes its place, the
  254D'   CD 0000*              	CALL	INSSTR		;   way WM splices one: the length byte with
                                				;   its string above it, 'u' capture included
  2550'                         SUBL_I:
  2550'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  2552'   32 29B9'              	STA	SLCHG
  2555'   3A 29B7'              	LDA	SGFLAG
  2558'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2559'   C2 24F9'              	JNZ	SUBL_L		; 'g': on from PAST the replacement
  255C'                         SUBL_D:
  255C'   CD 2563'              	CALL	SUBBOL
  255F'   3A 29B9'              	LDA	SLCHG
  2562'   C9                    	RET
                                
                                ;SUBBOL -- the cursor to the start of the line it is on.
  2563'                         SUBBOL:
  2563'   CD 0000*              	CALL	CUROFF
  2566'   CD 05D7'              	CALL	BOL
  2569'   C3 0000*              	JMP	GOTO
                                
                                ;VC_SRCH -- '/' and '?': read the pattern, then search.  C is the key, which
                                ; is both the prompt character and the direction.
  256C'                         VC_SRCH:
  256C'   CD 06EB'              	CALL	SETMOV
  256F'   C5                    	PUSH	B
  2570'   CD 067E'              	CALL	GETCNT
  2573'   22 29A9'              	SHLD	SCNT
  2576'   C1                    	POP	B
  2577'   79                    	MOV	A,C
  2578'   FE 2F                 	CPI	'/'
  257A'   3E 00                 	MVI	A,0
  257C'   CA 2581'              	JZ	VS_DIR
  257F'   3E FF                 	MVI	A,0FFH		; '?' -- backward
  2581'                         VS_DIR:
  2581'   32 297E'              	STA	SDIR
  2584'   11 0000*              	LXI	D,SRCHST	; the line is read straight into the pattern
  2587'   3A 0000*              	LDA	SRCMAX		; its capacity is BUF.MAC's to state
  258A'   47                    	MOV	B,A
  258B'   CD 1AD6'              	CALL	RDLINE
  258E'   B7                    	ORA	A
  258F'   C0                    	RNZ			; cancelled: nothing happens at all
  2590'   3A 2956'              	LDA	RLLEN
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-115


  2593'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2594'   CA 25A9'              	JZ	VS_OLD		; an empty line repeats the last pattern --
                                				;   RDLINE stored nothing, so it is still there
  2597'   5F                    	MOV	E,A
  2598'   16 00                 	MVI	D,0
  259A'   21 0000*              	LXI	H,SRCHST
  259D'   19                    	DAD	D
  259E'   36 00                 	MVI	M,0		; 0-terminated, and the length below it:
  25A0'   7B                    	MOV	A,E
  25A1'   32 0000*              	STA	SRCHBF		;   WM's RDSTR layout, built by hand
  25A4'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  25A6'   32 2980'              	STA	SHAVE
  25A9'                         VS_OLD:
  25A9'   3A 2980'              	LDA	SHAVE
  25AC'   B7                    	ORA	A
  25AD'   CA 25B9'              	JZ	VS_NOP		; '/' <CR> with nothing ever searched for
  25B0'   3A 297E'              	LDA	SDIR
  25B3'   32 297F'              	STA	SDIRE
  25B6'   C3 25E2'              	JMP	SRCHN
  25B9'                         VS_NOP:
  25B9'   21 225C'              	LXI	H,SNOPAT
  25BC'   C3 21BE'              	JMP	EXERR
                                
                                ;VC_N / VCB_N -- 'n' repeats the last search, 'N' reverses its direction.
  25BF'                         VC_N:
  25BF'   CD 06EB'              	CALL	SETMOV
  25C2'   3A 297E'              	LDA	SDIR
  25C5'   C3 25CF'              	JMP	VN_GO
  25C8'                         VCB_N:
  25C8'   CD 06EB'              	CALL	SETMOV
  25CB'   3A 297E'              	LDA	SDIR
  25CE'   2F                    	CMA			; the other way: 0 <-> FFH
  25CF'                         VN_GO:
  25CF'   32 297F'              	STA	SDIRE
  25D2'   CD 067E'              	CALL	GETCNT
  25D5'   22 29A9'              	SHLD	SCNT
  25D8'   3A 2980'              	LDA	SHAVE
  25DB'   B7                    	ORA	A
  25DC'   CA 25B9'              	JZ	VS_NOP
  25DF'   C3 25E2'              	JMP	SRCHN
                                
                                ;SRCHN -- the count, one whole-file search each.  Not found at ANY repeat
                                ; leaves the cursor where the command started, as vim does, and says so; the
                                ; message wants the screen left alone, which is what EXERR's R_MOVE is for.
  25E2'                         SRCHN:
  25E2'   CD 0EDF'              	CALL	GZONE		; the screen's top line, before the cursor moves
  25E5'   2A 0000*              	LHLD	LNDLT
  25E8'   22 29AE'              	SHLD	SLDLT		; the sweep moves the cursor with PUTCUR, which
                                				;   does not maintain LNDLT (GOTO does)
  25EB'   21 29AB'              	LXI	H,SOFF
  25EE'   CD 0442'              	CALL	FPSTO
  25F1'                         SN_LP:
  25F1'   CD 2626'              	CALL	SRCHGO
  25F4'   DA 260E'              	JC	SN_BAD
  25F7'   2A 29A9'              	LHLD	SCNT
  25FA'   2B                    	DCX	H
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-116


  25FB'   22 29A9'              	SHLD	SCNT
  25FE'   7C                    	MOV	A,H
  25FF'   B5                    	ORA	L
  2600'   C2 25F1'              	JNZ	SN_LP
  2603'   CD 0000*              	CALL	CLRCNT		; the sweep left CMDCNT wide open (and DIRFLG
                                				;   backward): WM clears both per command
  2606'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  2608'   32 298B'              	STA	GPKEEP		; land on the match, not the first non-blank
  260B'   C3 0EF7'              	JMP	GPLACE		; (which sets LNDLT itself)
  260E'                         SN_BAD:
  260E'   21 29AB'              	LXI	H,SOFF
  2611'   CD 03E9'              	CALL	BACKTO
  2614'   2A 29AE'              	LHLD	SLDLT
  2617'   22 0000*              	SHLD	LNDLT		; nothing moved: the row the cursor was on
  261A'   21 2248'              	LXI	H,SNOTFD
  261D'   CD 21BE'              	CALL	EXERR
  2620'   21 0000*              	LXI	H,SRCHST
  2623'   C3 21B9'              	JMP	EXOUT		; 'Pattern not found: ' and the pattern
                                
                                ;SRCHGO -- one search of the whole file, wrapping once: from the cursor to the
                                ; end in SDIRE's direction, then from the other end back to it.  CY = the
                                ; pattern is nowhere in the file (and the cursor is left at an end of it --
                                ; SRCHN is what puts it back).
  2626'                         SRCHGO:
  2626'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  2628'   32 29B0'              	STA	SSKIP		; this pass starts one past the cursor
  262B'   CD 263D'              	CALL	SRCHW
  262E'   D0                    	RNC
  262F'   3A 297F'              	LDA	SDIRE
  2632'   2F                    	CMA
  2633'   CD 0000*              	CALL	MVLMAX		; to the end the wrapped pass starts from
  2636'   AF                    	XRA	A
  2637'   32 29B0'              	STA	SSKIP		; ... but the wrapped pass starts AT it, or a
                                				;   match on the file's very first character
                                				;   would be stepped over and never found
  263A'   C3 263D'              	JMP	SRCHW
                                
                                ;SRCHW -- WM FINDA (WM 1943): the file from the cursor on, one resident
                                ; window at a time.  A miss moves the cursor to that window's edge and pages
                                ; one step (SCRLST), so the search runs off the window and through the file;
                                ; CY when the file runs out.  PAGESTP only pages while the count is unspent,
                                ; so the sweep opens CMDCNT wide, as MVLMAX does.  WM's FINDA then puts the
                                ; cursor exactly at the file's end (WM 1949); nothing here needs that -- the
                                ; caller either wraps to the other end or restores the cursor outright.
  263D'                         SRCHW:
  263D'   3A 297F'              	LDA	SDIRE
  2640'   32 0000*              	STA	DIRFLG
  2643'   21 FFFF               	LXI	H,0FFFFH
  2646'   22 28FE'              	SHLD	CMDCNT
  2649'   AF                    	XRA	A
  264A'   32 0000*              	STA	AUXCNT
  264D'                         SW_LP:
  264D'   CD 2659'              	CALL	SRCH1
  2650'   D0                    	RNC
  2651'   CD 0000*              	CALL	SCRLST
  2654'   D2 264D'              	JNC	SW_LP
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-117


  2657'   37                    	STC
  2658'   C9                    	RET
                                
                                ;SRCH1 -- WM FINDB (WM 1984) without its RDSTR: one search inside the
                                ; resident window, DIRFLG picking the direction.  CY = not in this window; on
                                ; a hit the cursor is ON the match's first character.
  2659'                         SRCH1:
  2659'   11 0000*              	LXI	D,SRCHST
  265C'   3A 0000*              	LDA	DIRFLG
  265F'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2660'   FA 2684'              	JM	SR1_BK
  2663'   21 0000*              	LXI	H,GAPEND
  2666'   CD 0000*              	CALL	FWDCNT
  2669'   2A 0000*              	LHLD	GAPEND		; WM loads the pointer BEFORE testing the
  266C'   D8                    	RC			;   carry: a miss still has to leave HL a
                                				;   real text address, because SCRLST puts
                                				;   the cursor there on the way to paging
  266D'   3A 29B0'              	LDA	SSKIP
  2670'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2671'   CA 267A'              	JZ	SR1_FW
  2674'   78                    	MOV	A,B
  2675'   B1                    	ORA	C
  2676'   37                    	STC
  2677'   C8                    	RZ			; no room for a match even before the skip
  2678'   0B                    	DCX	B		; vim's '/' starts one character PAST the
  2679'   23                    	INX	H		;   cursor, so the character under it can
                                				;   never be the one that matches
  267A'                         SR1_FW:
  267A'   CD 0000*              	CALL	MATCHF
  267D'   D8                    	RC
  267E'   CD 0000*              	CALL	SUBLEN		; MATCHF ends PAST the match; vi lands on it
  2681'   C3 0000*              	JMP	PUTCUR
  2684'                         SR1_BK:
  2684'   21 0000*              	LXI	H,TXTBEG
  2687'   CD 0000*              	CALL	FWDCNT
  268A'   2A 0000*              	LHLD	GAPBEG		; (as above: the pointer, then the carry)
  268D'   D8                    	RC
  268E'   CD 0000*              	CALL	SUBLEN		; MATCHB already lands on the first character
  2691'   CD 0000*              	CALL	MATCHB
  2694'   D8                    	RC
  2695'   C3 0000*              	JMP	PUTCUR
                                
                                ;============================================================
                                ; DISPATCH TABLES -- the messages first, then CMDTAB and ACTTAB.
                                ;============================================================
                                ;============================================================
                                ; MESSAGES -- the bottom row is vim's message line: blank unless something has
                                ; been said on it.  A message is built by successive calls -- EXPRM first, which
                                ; clears the row, then the pieces straight to the terminal.  There is no
                                ; concatenation buffer and none is wanted: the pieces are a name, a flag and a
                                ; number, and each knows how to write itself.
                                ;
                                ; Every message is drawn AFTER the frame (MSGPST, from the main loop), because
                                ; a full repaint blanks the bottom row -- so a message written during the
                                ; command would be wiped by the repaint that same key causes.
                                ;============================================================
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-118


                                
                                ;MSGNAM -- open the row and write the file name at HL (a 12-byte FCB field:
                                ; drive, 8 name, 3 type) in quotes, the way vim opens a message: "TEST.TXT".
                                ; A blank name field is vim's "[No Name]".  vim writes a path relative to the
                                ; current directory -- a file in it bare, "../x/VI.MAC" otherwise -- and the
                                ; logged drive is CP/M's current directory, so the drive is written only when
                                ; it is another one: "B:TEST.TXT".
  2698'                         MSGNAM:
  2698'   E5                    	PUSH	H
  2699'   3E 22                 	MVI	A,'"'
  269B'   CD 0000*              	CALL	EXPRM		; clears the row, writes the opening quote
  269E'   E1                    	POP	H
  269F'   E5                    	PUSH	H		; keep the base: the type field is 9 past it
  26A0'   23                    	INX	H
  26A1'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  26A2'   FE 20                 	CPI	' '
  26A4'   C2 26B1'              	JNZ	MN_HAS
  26A7'   21 22CA'              	LXI	H,SNONM
  26AA'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTSTR
  26AD'   E1                    	POP	H
  26AE'   C3 26FB'              	JMP	MN_END
  26B1'                         MN_HAS:
  26B1'   0E 19                 	MVI	C,BF_CURDSK
  26B3'   CD 0005               	CALL	BDOS		; A = the logged drive, A: = 0
  26B6'   E1                    	POP	H
  26B7'   E5                    	PUSH	H
  26B8'   3C                    	INR	A		; the FCB's drive byte is explicit, A: = 1
  26B9'   BE                    	CMP	M
  26BA'   CA 26C8'              	JZ	MN_NAM
  26BD'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  26BE'   C6 40                 	ADI	'A'-1
  26C0'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTCH
  26C3'   3E 3A                 	MVI	A,':'
  26C5'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTCH
  26C8'                         MN_NAM:
  26C8'   E1                    	POP	H
  26C9'   E5                    	PUSH	H
  26CA'   23                    	INX	H
  26CB'   0E 08                 	MVI	C,8
  26CD'                         MN_NM:
  26CD'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  26CE'   FE 20                 	CPI	' '
  26D0'   CA 26DB'              	JZ	MN_EXT
  26D3'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTCH
  26D6'   23                    	INX	H
  26D7'   0D                    	DCR	C
  26D8'   C2 26CD'              	JNZ	MN_NM
  26DB'                         MN_EXT:
  26DB'   E1                    	POP	H
  26DC'   11 0009               	LXI	D,9
  26DF'   19                    	DAD	D		; the type field
  26E0'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  26E1'   FE 20                 	CPI	' '
  26E3'   CA 26FB'              	JZ	MN_END
  26E6'   3E 2E                 	MVI	A,'.'
  26E8'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTCH
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-119


  26EB'   0E 03                 	MVI	C,3
  26ED'                         MN_XL:
  26ED'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  26EE'   FE 20                 	CPI	' '
  26F0'   CA 26FB'              	JZ	MN_END
  26F3'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTCH
  26F6'   23                    	INX	H
  26F7'   0D                    	DCR	C
  26F8'   C2 26ED'              	JNZ	MN_XL
  26FB'                         MN_END:
  26FB'   3E 22                 	MVI	A,'"'
  26FD'   C3 0000*              	JMP	OUTCH
                                
                                ;MSGFLG -- the flags after the name, in vim's order and with vim's spelling:
                                ; [Modified][readonly][New], run together with ONE space before the first of
                                ; them (measured from vim 9.1).  A = 0 leaves [Modified] out, which is the load
                                ; message, where nothing has been changed yet.
  2700'                         MSGFLG:
  2700'   06 00                 	MVI	B,0		; B: a flag has been written (the space goes once)
  2702'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2703'   CA 2713'              	JZ	MF_RO
  2706'   3A 2901'              	LDA	MODF
  2709'   B7                    	ORA	A
  270A'   CA 2713'              	JZ	MF_RO
  270D'   21 22D4'              	LXI	H,SMODM
  2710'   CD 2728'              	CALL	MF_PUT
  2713'                         MF_RO:
  2713'   3A 296C'              	LDA	ROFLAG
  2716'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2717'   CA 2720'              	JZ	MF_NEW
  271A'   21 22DF'              	LXI	H,SROM
  271D'   CD 2728'              	CALL	MF_PUT
  2720'                         MF_NEW:
  2720'   3A 296A'              	LDA	MSGNEW
  2723'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2724'   C8                    	RZ
  2725'   21 22EA'              	LXI	H,SNEWM
  2728'                         MF_PUT:
  2728'   78                    	MOV	A,B
  2729'   B7                    	ORA	A
  272A'   C2 2736'              	JNZ	MF_STR
  272D'   06 01                 	MVI	B,1
  272F'   E5                    	PUSH	H
  2730'   3E 20                 	MVI	A,' '
  2732'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTCH
  2735'   E1                    	POP	H
  2736'                         MF_STR:
  2736'   C3 0000*              	JMP	OUTSTR
                                
                                ;EMITW -- HL as unsigned decimal, no leading zeros (0 still prints one digit).
                                ; Repeated subtraction of each power of ten, which is all an 8080 has.  B
                                ; carries 'a digit has been printed' so the leading zeros are skipped.
  2739'                         EMITW:
  2739'   C5                    	PUSH	B
  273A'   D5                    	PUSH	D
  273B'   06 00                 	MVI	B,0
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-120


  273D'   11 2710               	LXI	D,10000
  2740'   CD 275E'              	CALL	EWDIG
  2743'   11 03E8               	LXI	D,1000
  2746'   CD 275E'              	CALL	EWDIG
  2749'   11 0064               	LXI	D,100
  274C'   CD 275E'              	CALL	EWDIG
  274F'   11 000A               	LXI	D,10
  2752'   CD 275E'              	CALL	EWDIG
  2755'   7D                    	MOV	A,L		; the units digit is always printed
  2756'   C6 30                 	ADI	'0'
  2758'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTCH
  275B'   D1                    	POP	D
  275C'   C1                    	POP	B
  275D'   C9                    	RET
  275E'                         EWDIG:
  275E'   0E 00                 	MVI	C,0
  2760'                         EW_LP:
  2760'   7D                    	MOV	A,L
  2761'   93                    	SUB	E
  2762'   6F                    	MOV	L,A
  2763'   7C                    	MOV	A,H
  2764'   9A                    	SBB	D
  2765'   67                    	MOV	H,A
  2766'   DA 276D'              	JC	EW_DN
  2769'   0C                    	INR	C
  276A'   C3 2760'              	JMP	EW_LP
  276D'                         EW_DN:
  276D'   19                    	DAD	D		; one subtraction too many: give it back
  276E'   79                    	MOV	A,C
  276F'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2770'   C2 2776'              	JNZ	EW_PR
  2773'   78                    	MOV	A,B
  2774'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2775'   C8                    	RZ			; a leading zero: write nothing
  2776'                         EW_PR:
  2776'   79                    	MOV	A,C
  2777'   C6 30                 	ADI	'0'
  2779'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTCH
  277C'   06 01                 	MVI	B,1
  277E'   C9                    	RET
                                
                                ;ISEMPT -- Z iff the buffer holds nothing at all: no resident text, and nothing
                                ; spilled behind the window either.  vim's '--No lines in buffer--', shortened.
  277F'                         ISEMPT:
  277F'   2A 0000*              	LHLD	OUTPOS
  2782'   7C                    	MOV	A,H
  2783'   B5                    	ORA	L
  2784'   C0                    	RNZ
  2785'   CD 0000*              	CALL	LOGLEN
  2788'   7C                    	MOV	A,H
  2789'   B5                    	ORA	L
  278A'   C9                    	RET
                                
                                ;MSGCTG -- '^G': the file, its flags, and where the cursor is.  DEVIATION: vim
                                ; also gives the file's line total and a percentage of it; no line total is kept
                                ; anywhere here, and counting one means a sweep of the whole file out to EOF and
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-121


                                ; a page back -- which would cost the undo region (PGTOPM) as well as the wait.
  278B'                         MSGCTG:
  278B'   21 0000*              	LXI	H,FNAME
  278E'   CD 2698'              	CALL	MSGNAM
  2791'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1		; '^G' is where vim shows [Modified]
  2793'   CD 2700'              	CALL	MSGFLG
  2796'   CD 277F'              	CALL	ISEMPT
  2799'   C2 27A2'              	JNZ	MG_POS
  279C'   21 22FD'              	LXI	H,SNOLIN
  279F'   C3 0000*              	JMP	OUTSTR
  27A2'                         MG_POS:
  27A2'   21 22F0'              	LXI	H,SLINE
  27A5'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTSTR
  27A8'   CD 0000*              	CALL	CNTLF		; the line feeds before the cursor ...
  27AB'   23                    	INX	H		;   ... so the line it is on
  27AC'   CD 2739'              	CALL	EMITW
  27AF'   21 22F7'              	LXI	H,SCOLM
  27B2'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTSTR
  27B5'   2A 0000*              	LHLD	CBOL		; the byte column within the line, 0-based
  27B8'   23                    	INX	H
  27B9'   E5                    	PUSH	H
  27BA'   CD 2739'              	CALL	EMITW
  27BD'   2A 0000*              	LHLD	CURDCL		; the column a TAB expands that byte to
  27C0'   23                    	INX	H
  27C1'   D1                    	POP	D
  27C2'   CD 0000*              	CALL	CMP16		; the same -> vim writes one number, not two
  27C5'   C8                    	RZ
  27C6'   E5                    	PUSH	H
  27C7'   3E 2D                 	MVI	A,'-'
  27C9'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTCH
  27CC'   E1                    	POP	H
  27CD'   C3 2739'              	JMP	EMITW
                                
                                ;FILMSG -- the load message: the file and its flags.  vim adds the line and
                                ; byte totals ('3L, 30B'); the line total is the sweep MSGCTG's note describes.
                                ; A buffer with no file name gets no message at all, as in vim.
  27D0'                         FILMSG:
  27D0'   3A 0001*              	LDA	FNAME+1
  27D3'   FE 20                 	CPI	' '
  27D5'   C8                    	RZ
  27D6'   21 0000*              	LXI	H,FNAME
  27D9'   CD 2698'              	CALL	MSGNAM
  27DC'   AF                    	XRA	A		; nothing has been changed yet
  27DD'   C3 2700'              	JMP	MSGFLG
                                
                                ;WRMSG -- what ':w' says.  vim counts what it wrote ('"TEST.TXT" 3L, 29B
                                ; written'); the counts are the same sweep, so this says only that it happened.
                                ; The name is TFCB's, so ':w OTHER.TXT' names the file it really wrote.
  27E0'                         WRMSG:
  27E0'   21 0000*              	LXI	H,TFCB
  27E3'   CD 2698'              	CALL	MSGNAM
  27E6'   21 230F'              	LXI	H,SWRIT
  27E9'   C3 0000*              	JMP	OUTSTR
                                
                                ;MSGPST -- draw the message this key asked for, once the frame is painted, and
                                ; put the cursor back.  The main loop calls it after SCDRAW and MODEMS.
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-122


  27EC'                         MSGPST:
  27EC'   3A 2969'              	LDA	MSGWHT
  27EF'   B7                    	ORA	A
  27F0'   C8                    	RZ
  27F1'   47                    	MOV	B,A
  27F2'   AF                    	XRA	A
  27F3'   32 2969'              	STA	MSGWHT
  27F6'   78                    	MOV	A,B
  27F7'   FE 01                 	CPI	MW_FIL
  27F9'   CA 2815'              	JZ	MP_FIL
  27FC'   FE 02                 	CPI	MW_WRT
  27FE'   CA 281B'              	JZ	MP_WRT
  2801'   FE 04                 	CPI	MW_BIG
  2803'   CA 280C'              	JZ	MP_BIG
  2806'   CD 278B'              	CALL	MSGCTG
  2809'   C3 0000*              	JMP	SCURS
  280C'                         MP_BIG:
  280C'   21 22AB'              	LXI	H,SQBIG
  280F'   CD 0000*              	CALL	EXMSG
  2812'   C3 0000*              	JMP	SCURS
  2815'                         MP_FIL:
  2815'   CD 27D0'              	CALL	FILMSG
  2818'   C3 0000*              	JMP	SCURS
  281B'                         MP_WRT:
  281B'   CD 27E0'              	CALL	WRMSG
  281E'   C3 0000*              	JMP	SCURS
                                
                                ;VC_CTLG -- '^G'.  It changes nothing and moves nothing, so the frame is a
                                ; still one and the message goes on after it.
  2821'                         VC_CTLG:
  2821'   3E 03                 	MVI	A,MW_CTG
  2823'   32 2969'              	STA	MSGWHT
  2826'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,R_MOVE
  2828'   32 2902'              	STA	RINTENT
  282B'   C9                    	RET
                                
  282C'                         CMDTAB:
  282C'   07                    	DB	07H			; ^G -- where am I?
  282D'   2821'                 	DW	VC_CTLG
  282F'   68                    	DB	'h'
  2830'   0744'                 	DW	VC_H
  2832'   6C                    	DB	'l'
  2833'   076E'                 	DW	VC_L
  2835'   6A                    	DB	'j'
  2836'   0833'                 	DW	VC_J
  2838'   6B                    	DB	'k'
  2839'   0842'                 	DW	VC_K
  283B'   30                    	DB	'0'
  283C'   0795'                 	DW	VC_0
  283E'   5E                    	DB	'^'
  283F'   07B3'                 	DW	VC_CRT
  2841'   24                    	DB	'$'
  2842'   07F1'                 	DW	VC_EOL
  2844'   0D                    	DB	CR			; <CR> -- down to the first non-blank
  2845'   0815'                 	DW	VC_CR
  2847'   77                    	DB	'w'
